1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:01,821
P. Govindasamy Pillai:

2
00:00:01,901 --> 00:00:05,875
From Shop Boy to Serangoon
Road Retail Pioneer

3
00:00:05,875 --> 00:00:11,006
In 1905 a teenage runaway from
a village in South India stepped

4
00:00:11,086 --> 00:00:16,875
ashore at Tanjong Pagar with 13
rupees and the clothes he was wearing.

5
00:00:16,875 --> 00:00:20,301
He found work in a provision
shop on Serangoon Road,

6
00:00:20,381 --> 00:00:24,751
where he was paid in meals and a
place to sleep rather than wages,

7
00:00:24,831 --> 00:00:28,000
and twenty-four years
later he bought the shop.

8
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,403
P. Govindasamy Pillai, popularly
known as PGP, went on to build

9
00:00:32,483 --> 00:00:35,889
a chain of stores that
reached Malacca and Johor,

10
00:00:35,969 --> 00:00:40,950
and he gave much of what he earned
to temples, missions and hospitals.

11
00:00:40,950 --> 00:00:44,840
The stores that carried his
initials closed in 1998,

12
00:00:44,920 --> 00:00:48,352
and his name is less
familiar today than those

13
00:00:48,432 --> 00:00:52,325
of the Chinese business
families of his generation.

14
00:00:52,325 --> 00:00:56,030
Pillai was born in 1887
and grew up in Koorainadu,

15
00:00:56,110 --> 00:01:00,876
a village near the town of
Mayavaram, now called Mayiladuthurai,

16
00:01:00,956 --> 00:01:05,725
in what was then the Tanjore
district of the Madras Presidency.

17
00:01:05,725 --> 00:01:09,590
In his teens he ran away
from home and boarded a

18
00:01:09,670 --> 00:01:14,025
ship for Singapore, landing
at Tanjong Pagar in 1905.

19
00:01:14,025 --> 00:01:17,134
After many failed attempts to find a job,

20
00:01:17,214 --> 00:01:21,725
he was taken on at a provision
store at 50 Serangoon Road.

21
00:01:21,725 --> 00:01:25,542
The work started before dawn
and ended after midnight,

22
00:01:25,622 --> 00:01:29,375
and he was paid no wages,
only his food and lodging.

23
00:01:29,375 --> 00:01:32,959
It was in this shop, according
to the National Library's

24
00:01:33,039 --> 00:01:36,900
account of his life, that he
learned how to run a business.

25
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:39,750
He stayed for more than twenty years.

26
00:01:39,750 --> 00:01:43,534
In 1929 he went back to
India and returned to

27
00:01:43,614 --> 00:01:48,250
Singapore with his wife,
Pakiriammal, who was then 15.

28
00:01:48,250 --> 00:01:52,445
He came back to find himself
without a job, because the owner of

29
00:01:52,525 --> 00:01:57,000
the provision store had died and
the shop had been put up for sale.

30
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,675
Pillai decided to buy it.

31
00:01:59,675 --> 00:02:03,316
He borrowed $2,000 from the
Chettiars, the South Indian

32
00:02:03,396 --> 00:02:07,646
moneylenders who lent to traders
across the Straits Settlements,

33
00:02:07,726 --> 00:02:11,650
and took over the shop he had
worked in since his arrival.

34
00:02:11,650 --> 00:02:14,876
He started by selling
spices, oils and grains,

35
00:02:14,956 --> 00:02:18,326
then widened the range
to everything from canned

36
00:02:18,406 --> 00:02:22,000
goods to shoes, and opened
more shops in the area.

37
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,825
Customers came to know
the business by his name.

38
00:02:25,825 --> 00:02:28,132
Pillai was known for his frugality,

39
00:02:28,212 --> 00:02:31,350
and he put his savings
back into the business.

40
00:02:31,350 --> 00:02:34,952
He opened textile shops
and flour and spice mills,

41
00:02:35,032 --> 00:02:39,450
and his saree shops became the
best known part of the trade.

42
00:02:39,450 --> 00:02:43,997
His staff worked from 7 a.m. to
about 9 p.m., with a two-hour

43
00:02:44,077 --> 00:02:48,400
break at lunch, and he gave
them lodging in his own home.

44
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:51,690
By the late 1930s he
was one of the leading

45
00:02:51,770 --> 00:02:55,375
figures in Singapore's
South Indian community.

46
00:02:55,375 --> 00:02:58,956
He had helped found the
Ramakrishna Mission in

47
00:02:59,036 --> 00:03:02,855
Singapore in 1928, and he
was a founder-member of

48
00:03:02,935 --> 00:03:06,675
the Indian Chamber of
Commerce, set up in 1937.

49
00:03:06,675 --> 00:03:11,550
In 1939 he was appointed
a Justice of the Peace.

50
00:03:11,550 --> 00:03:14,364
According to the Singapore Memory Project,

51
00:03:14,444 --> 00:03:19,462
the appointment recognised his work
in rallying the South Indian community

52
00:03:19,542 --> 00:03:23,125
behind the war effort after
war broke out in Europe.

53
00:03:23,125 --> 00:03:27,395
When Japan invaded Malaya in
December 1941, Pillai and his

54
00:03:27,475 --> 00:03:32,200
family were in India, and they
spent the rest of the war there.

55
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:35,927
They were spared the Japanese
Occupation of Singapore,

56
00:03:36,007 --> 00:03:38,475
but he lost his property and goods.

57
00:03:38,475 --> 00:03:41,362
He returned in 1945 and started the

58
00:03:41,442 --> 00:03:44,325
business again from the beginning.

59
00:03:44,325 --> 00:03:47,525
This time it grew larger than before.

60
00:03:47,525 --> 00:03:49,998
He expanded across the Causeway,

61
00:03:50,078 --> 00:03:52,950
opening stores in Malacca and Johor.

62
00:03:52,950 --> 00:03:57,345
The Malacca shops were at first
called Dhanalakshmi Stores,

63
00:03:57,425 --> 00:03:59,317
after his eldest daughter,

64
00:03:59,397 --> 00:04:04,100
and later renamed P. Govindasamy
Pillai Stores, or PGP Stores.

65
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:07,545
He also invested in
property on Serangoon Road,

66
00:04:07,625 --> 00:04:11,370
Race Course Road and Buffalo
Road, and built houses

67
00:04:11,450 --> 00:04:14,975
in Campbell Lane and
later in Race Course Road.

68
00:04:14,975 --> 00:04:17,692
Pillai was a devout Hindu, and he gave

69
00:04:17,772 --> 00:04:20,275
away a great deal of what he made.

70
00:04:20,275 --> 00:04:24,293
In 1952 the Ramakrishna
Mission moved into a new

71
00:04:24,373 --> 00:04:28,300
home at Bartley Road,
built with his donation.

72
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:31,674
He went to the opening of
its new temple himself,

73
00:04:31,754 --> 00:04:36,125
and handed out drinks, cakes and
sweets to the children there.

74
00:04:36,125 --> 00:04:41,204
He also gave to the University of
Malaya and to Mount Alvernia Hospital,

75
00:04:41,284 --> 00:04:43,498
and he sat on a Singapore Indian

76
00:04:43,578 --> 00:04:47,582
Association committee raising
funds for a home for people

77
00:04:47,662 --> 00:04:51,675
who could no longer work
because of sickness or old age.

78
00:04:51,675 --> 00:04:54,407
His largest gifts went to the Sri

79
00:04:54,487 --> 00:04:58,150
Srinivasa Perumal Temple
on Serangoon Road.

80
00:04:58,150 --> 00:05:02,313
In the early 1960s he paid
for much of its redevelopment,

81
00:05:02,393 --> 00:05:08,125
including a wedding hall, one of the
first Indian wedding halls in Singapore.

82
00:05:08,125 --> 00:05:12,743
Named PGP Hall, it was opened
on the 19th of June 1965 by

83
00:05:12,823 --> 00:05:16,781
Yusof bin Ishak, then the
Yang di-Pertuan Negara,

84
00:05:16,861 --> 00:05:22,300
and the first wedding held there
was that of his son Ramakrishnan.

85
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:25,593
The temple's present five-tier gopuram,

86
00:05:25,673 --> 00:05:30,349
completed in 1979, was built
with $300,000 from Pillai,

87
00:05:30,429 --> 00:05:33,975
according to the National Heritage Board.

88
00:05:33,975 --> 00:05:39,475
The temple was gazetted as
a national monument in 1978.

89
00:05:39,475 --> 00:05:43,703
Pillai retired in 1963 and
handed the family business,

90
00:05:43,783 --> 00:05:47,133
then valued at 3 million
Singapore dollars,

91
00:05:47,213 --> 00:05:51,600
to his children, and his sons
expanded the saree trade.

92
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:54,744
He died of a heart attack on the

93
00:05:54,824 --> 00:05:58,450
21st of July 1980, at the age of 93.

94
00:05:58,450 --> 00:06:00,913
After the death of his youngest son,

95
00:06:00,993 --> 00:06:07,202
Pillai's daughter-in-law managed the PGP
stores, including the saree shop and supermarket

96
00:06:07,282 --> 00:06:11,450
that stood on the site now
occupied by Little India Arcade.

97
00:06:11,450 --> 00:06:16,650
The stores ran into heavy
debts and closed in 1998.

98
00:06:16,650 --> 00:06:20,375
His name has lasted
longer than his stores.

99
00:06:20,375 --> 00:06:25,300
PGP Hall still stands in the
grounds of the Perumal temple.

100
00:06:25,300 --> 00:06:30,789
Singapore Post issued stamps featuring
him on the 28th of February 2001,

101
00:06:30,869 --> 00:06:34,191
and in 2019 he was one
of the eight pioneers

102
00:06:34,271 --> 00:06:40,147
pictured on the back of the $20 note
issued to mark Singapore's bicentennial,

103
00:06:40,227 --> 00:06:43,475
alongside Tan Kah Kee and Munshi Abdullah.

104
00:06:43,475 --> 00:06:48,260
Why it matters today: Singapore's
best-remembered business pioneers

105
00:06:48,340 --> 00:06:52,625
are mostly Chinese towkays,
but Serangoon Road had its own.

106
00:06:52,625 --> 00:06:56,692
A shop hand who worked for his
meals built one of the best-known

107
00:06:56,772 --> 00:07:01,164
Indian businesses of his time, and
the mission home, wedding hall and

108
00:07:01,244 --> 00:07:05,650
temple tower he paid for still stand
on the streets where he worked.
